# Big-O

## What Is Big O?
Big O notation describes how the runtime or memory usage of an alrgorightm scales as the size of the input grows.

Time Complexity: The number of operations that it takes to complete as the input increases

Space Complexity: The amount of space in memory an algorithm needs to run to completion as the function of the input increases.

## Big O Worst Case
### Omega:
Best case
### Theta:
Average Case
### O (Omicron):
Worst Case 

## O(n)
As the amount of data increases, the amount of work increases at the same rate. 
This is also called linear time.

example code:
```
void printItems(int n) {
    for (int i= 0; i < n; i++) {
        cout << i << endl;
    }
}
```
### O(n^2)
As the amount of data increases, the amount of work grows at the rate of the input size (n) multiplied by itself (n)
It grows much more faster than O(n) making it less efficient. 
So if I were to choose a function where one runs in O(n^2) then the other O(n), I would pick O(n).

example code:
```
void printItems(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++){
            cout << i << j << endl;
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    printItems(10)
}
```
The example code should run n * n which is where we get n^2.



